<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Baldwin I, Latin Emperor</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1172">1172</a>–<a href="/years/1205">1205</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Baldwin IX of Flanders, Baldwin VI of Hainaut, Baldwin I of Constantinople</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Baldwin I, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> Emperor, was count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a> and Hainaut and a leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourth-crusade">Fourth Crusade</a>. After the crusaders took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in 1204 he was elected the first emperor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-empire">Latin Empire</a>. In April 1205 he was defeated and captured by the Bulgarian tsar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaloyan-of-bulgaria">Kaloyan</a> at Adrianople and died in captivity; his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-of-flanders">Henry of Flanders</a> succeeded him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/baldwin-i-latin-emperor.jpg" alt="Baldwin I, Latin Emperor" width="238" height="395" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: maître de fauvel · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Baldwin_I_of_Constantinople.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Baldwin was the son of Count Baldwin V of Hainaut and Countess Margaret I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a>. In 1186 he married Marie, daughter of Count Henry I of Champagne, who was a niece of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-i-of-england">Richard I of England</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-france">Philip II of France</a>. Through her he had ties to the defenders of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-land">Holy Land</a>, and his uncle Count Philip I of Flanders had died on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> at the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acre">Acre</a> in 1191. Baldwin became Count of Flanders in 1194 and Count of Hainaut in 1195.</p><p>His eight years in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a> were taken up with recovering lands, including Artois, that had gone to the French crown as dowry. The Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peronne">Péronne</a>, signed in January 1200, made him a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of Philip II and returned parts of Artois to him. On 23 February 1200, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bruges">Bruges</a>, Baldwin took the cross, and he left for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourth-crusade">Fourth Crusade</a> on 14 April 1202.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a>'s leaders, short of money, were persuaded by the Venetians to divert to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, and the city fell in April 1204. The crown was first offered to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enrico-dandolo">Enrico Dandolo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/doge-of-venice">Doge of Venice</a>, who refused it. The choice then lay between Baldwin and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boniface-i-marquis-of-montferrat">Boniface of Montferrat</a>, the nominal leader of the crusade. With Venetian support Baldwin was elected on 9 May 1204 and crowned on 16 May in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hagia-sophia">Hagia Sophia</a>, in a ceremony that closely followed Byzantine practice.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-empire">Latin Empire</a> was organised on feudal lines. His own share included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> and nearby regions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>, with the islands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lemnos">Lemnos</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lesbos">Lesbos</a>, Chios and Tenos. He quarrelled with Boniface, who received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessalonica</a>, before Dandolo and Count Louis I of Blois arranged a settlement. In February 1205 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a> rose, relying on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaloyan-of-bulgaria">Kaloyan</a>, tsar of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>, whose offer of alliance Baldwin had rejected.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>At Adrianople on 14 April 1205 the Frankish knights were defeated, the count of Blois was killed and Baldwin was taken. His fate was uncertain for more than a year; reliable word of his death reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> only in July 1206, and his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-of-flanders">Henry of Flanders</a> was crowned emperor in August. Accounts of his end differ. A tower at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veliko-tarnovo">Veliko Tarnovo</a> is still called Baldwin's Tower.</p><p>In 1225 a man appeared in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a> claiming to be Baldwin and drew followers among the peasantry. He was unmasked as a Burgundian serf, Bertrand of Ray, and executed in 1226. Baldwin's daughters Joan and Margaret II both later ruled as countesses of Flanders.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Baldwin I, Latin Emperor” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Count Philip of Alsace died at the siege of Acre in 1191 during the Third Crusade, and <b>Baldwin IX</b> was a leader of the Fourth Crusade, which took Constantinople in 1204.</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-flanders">County of Flanders · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Mons and Valenciennes were reunited in the eleventh century, and the marriage in 1051 of Countess Richilde, widow of Count Hermann of Mons, to Baldwin of Flanders, later <b>Baldwin VI</b>, brought the county under a branch of the Flemish comital house.</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-hainaut">County of Hainaut · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>On 9 May 1204 <b>Baldwin IX of Flanders</b> was elected emperor with Venetian support and crowned as Baldwin I on 16 May in the Hagia Sophia, in a ceremony that closely followed Eastern Roman practice.</blockquote><a href="/empire/latin-empire">Latin Empire · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valenciennes">Valenciennes</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veliko-tarnovo">Veliko Tarnovo</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.36,3.52&amp;year=1193" aria-label="Valenciennes on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4630 414 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Baldwin I, Latin Emperor"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5080 684h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5080 684h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=50.36,3.52&amp;year=1193">What was here in 1193? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
